.TH egrep "" "Command"
.SH NAME
egrep \- extended pattern search
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBegrep [\-bcefhlnsvy] [\fIpattern\fB] [\fIfile ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
egrep -- extended pattern search
Usage:	egrep [ option ... ] [ pattern ] [ file ... ]
Options:
	-b	Each output line has the block number of the match
	-c	Print only a count of the number of matching lines
	-e	The next argument is the pattern
	-f	The next argument is a file containing one pattern per line
	-h	Suppress the printing of file names on matched lines
	-l	Print only the names of files containing matches
	-n	Print line number of file with each matched line output
	-s	Suppress output, just return status
	-v	Negate the sense of the match
	-y	Lower-case letters in the pattern match upper- and lower-case
The 'pattern' is a pattern roughly like that found in 'ed'.
If no 'file' is specified, the standard input is used.
egrep is like 'grep -a', but is an order of magnitude faster.
.HE
.B egrep
searches each
.I file
for lines matching
.IR pattern .
If no
.I file
is given, it searches the standard input.
Normally each matching line is printed.
Unlike
.BR grep ,
.B egrep
always accepts the extended metacharacters
.BR ( ,
.BR ) ,
.BR | ,
.BR + ,
and
.B ?
described below, and it builds a deterministic finite automaton from
the pattern before scanning any input, which is why it runs
considerably faster than
.B grep
on long input.
.PP
Exactly one
.I pattern
must be supplied, either as the first non-option argument, with
.BR \-e ,
or as the contents of the file named by
.BR \-f ;
these three sources are mutually exclusive.
.SH Patterns
A
.I pattern
is built from ordinary characters, which match themselves, and the
following:
.IP \fB^\fR 0.4i
Anchors the match to the start of the line.
.IP \fB$\fR
Anchors the match to the end of the line.
.IP \fB.\fR
Matches any single character.
.IP \fB*\fR
Matches zero or more occurrences of the preceding pattern element.
.IP \fB+\fR
Matches one or more occurrences of the preceding pattern element.
.IP \fB?\fR
Matches zero or one occurrence of the preceding pattern element.
.IP \fB[\fIchars\fB]\fR
Matches any one of the enclosed characters; a leading
.B ^
complements the set, and ranges may be written with
.BR \- .
.IP \fB\e\fIc\fR
Matches the character
.I c
literally.
.IP "\fB(\fIpattern\fB)\fR"
Groups a subpattern, for use with
.BR * ,
.BR + ,
.BR ? ,
or
.BR | .
.IP \fB|\fR
Matches either the pattern before it or the pattern after it.
A newline embedded in the
.I pattern
argument acts the same as
.BR | .
.PP
Because several of these characters are also special to the shell,
a
.I pattern
that uses them should be quoted.
.SH Options
.IP \fB\-b\fR 0.4i
Precede each matching line with the number of the input block it
started in.
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Print only a count of matching lines, not the lines themselves.
.IP \fB\-e\fR
The next argument is
.IR pattern ,
useful when it begins with
.BR \- .
.IP \fB\-f\fR
The next argument names a file containing one pattern per line;
no
.I pattern
argument is given on the command line.
.IP \fB\-h\fR
Do not print file names ahead of matched lines.
.IP \fB\-l\fR
Print only the names of files that contain a match.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Precede each matching line with its line number within its file.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Print nothing; only set the exit status.
.IP \fB\-v\fR
Print lines that do
.I not
match
.IR pattern .
.IP \fB\-y\fR
Make lower-case letters in
.I pattern
match either case in the input.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR awk ,
.BR ed ,
.BR expr ,
.B grep
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B egrep
returns 0 if any line matched, 1 if none did, and 2 if invoked
incorrectly or a file could not be opened.
